{
  "_note": "THE STATE-MACHINE INPUT PLANE — the fold that turns a bound `inputs` object into the smallest set of writes a Rive state machine needs. THE LAW. (1) A machine declares its inputs and their types (boolean | number | trigger); markup binds VALUES. The declared list is authority: a bound key the machine does not declare is refused (`unknown_input`) and never guessed at. (2) A boolean takes a bool, the strings 'true'/'false' (trimmed, case-insensitive) or a number (0 is false); a number takes a finite number or a numeric string, and REFUSES a boolean, because `count: true` is a shape mistake worth naming, not a 1. (3) LEVELS ARE DIFFED: an unchanged boolean or number produces NO op, so a re-render that changed nothing does not churn the machine. (4) A TRIGGER IS AN EDGE, never a level: it fires when its bound value CHANGES and the new value is truthy ('', 'false' and '0' are the falsy tokens). That is what makes `inputs=\"{{ { celebrate: order.justPlaced } }}\"` fire once per order instead of once per frame, and it is why the fold carries `values` forward: the previous token map IS the edge detector. (5) A REFUSAL NEVER ABORTS THE PLAN — the other inputs still apply, because one bad binding must not freeze an animation. (6) ORDER IS DETERMINISTIC: ops follow the machine's DECLARED order (never a hash map's), and unknown-name refusals follow sorted order, so three renderers emit the same list. Function: riveInputPlan(declared, bound, previous) on all three.",
